diff --git a/MIGRATION.md b/MIGRATION.md
index ce56a74c4..38ce40da3 100644
--- a/MIGRATION.md
+++ b/MIGRATION.md
@@ -5,6 +5,131 @@ records the breaking changes that landed in that cut, newest first. Clean breaks
 — no legacy aliases, no back-compat shims (L invariant 4); every break is a one-line
 rename or import re-point per call site.
 
+## Unreleased — the selectable tile's floor
+
+Four bindings leave. Each is a one-line change per call site.
+
+_`<Card>` loses `selected`_
+
+A card is content, never an option: `selected` and everything it stamped (`role="option"`,
+`tabindex="0"`, `aria-selected`, `data-selected`, the selected fill, hover and press) are
+gone. A set of choices is a `<ToggleGroup type="single">`. A card that a listbox walks by
+`aria-activedescendant` passes `role="option"` and `aria-selected` as attributes, which
+now reach the plate untouched. Detector: `rg ':selected=|\bselected\b' -g '*.vue'` on
+`<Card` lines; `vue-tsc` flags a typed site.
+
+_`<Chip shape="cell">` leaves, and an unknown `shape` throws_
+
+The shapes are `pill` and `icon`. A chip given any other shape throws a `TypeError` naming
+the shapes (`[Chip] shape="cell" is not one of pill, icon.`), in production as well as in
+development; it reaches your app's error handler (the chip itself still renders, as a
+pill). A selectable option that holds more than one line is a `ToggleGroup` item.
+Detector: `rg 'shape="cell"'`.
+
+_`<SegmentedTabs semantics="toggle">` becomes `semantics="radio"`, and an unknown
+`semantics` throws_
+
+The non-tabs semantic is a one-of-N chooser and now says so: `role="radiogroup"` with
+`role="radio"` + `aria-checked` items (was `role="group"` with `aria-pressed`), the
+answer `<ToggleGroup type="single">` gives. Every arrow moves. The default pairing is
+unchanged (pill → `radio`, underline → `tabs`). A value other than `radio` or `tabs`
+throws `[SegmentedTabs] semantics="toggle" is not one of radio, tabs.` in production as
+well as in development, the same refusal Chip gives an unknown `shape`; the strip still
+renders on the default pairing. Detector: `rg 'semantics="toggle"'`, and the error.
+
+_A `role`, `tabindex` or selection state on a `ToggleGroupItem` no longer replaces the
+group's_
+
+The group's engine owns each option's `role`, `aria-checked` / `aria-pressed` /
+`aria-selected` and roving `tabindex`. A consumer value used to win and broke the
+one-tab-stop radio group (`option` inside a `radiogroup`, one stop per item); it is now
+dropped, in production as in development. In a `type="multiple"` group the option's role
+is its button's own, so a consumer `role` is dropped there too. Every other attribute
+(class, style, `data-*`, `aria-label`, `title`, listeners) still lands on the button.
+Delete the five. Detector: in a development build, `[ToggleGroupItem] value=… sets role,
+tabindex: the group's engine owns an option's role, state and tab stop …`;
+`rg 'ToggleGroupItem[^>]*(role=|tabindex=|aria-(checked|pressed|selected)=)'`.
+
+_Behaviour and paint, nothing to change_
+
+- `<FadingScroll>` is a tab stop only while its content holds none. A `ToggleGroup` (or
+  any focusable content) inside it used to read two stops, the port and then the
+  content. The port takes `tabindex="0"` only while nothing inside it is a tab stop (an
+  element at `tabindex="-1"` or a disabled control is not one), and re-reads when its
+  content changes; a port of focusable content scrolls as focus moves through it.
+- `<ToggleGroup>` pads its row by `--focus-ring-reach` (the ring's outline plus offset,
+  now a token), so a scroll port around it cannot cut the focus ring. Rows grow by
+  4px on each side.
+- `<ToggleGroup>` moves on every arrow: Up and Down go to the nearest item in the next
+  row, and step in order where there is none. After a keyed reorder the arrows follow
+  the new order. An arrow typed into an input inside an item stays the input's.
+- A `ToggleGroup` pill and a `SegmentedTabs` tab hold one row. The pill clips at the
+  row, ellipsizes each label element and is clamped to its rung, and lays its direct
+  content out in a row whatever classes it carries. Inside a pill or a tab a `<br>` is
+  dropped and every element runs inline, whether the browser lays it out as a block (a
+  `div`, a `p`) or a class or an inline style sets its display (`block`, `flex`,
+  `style="display: block"`), so two lines written into a label read as one row. An
+  `svg` or `img` runs inline at its own size, and a wrapper that was `flex` or
+  `inline-flex` runs inline, so its `gap` no longer spaces its children. Three things
+  keep their own display: a `Badge`, which sits on the row as one inline box; an
+  element hidden by the `hidden` attribute, by `v-show` or by the `hidden` utility;
+  and an inline `display: … !important`, the one spelling that outranks the row. In a
+  development build of your app a `ToggleGroupItem` whose content asks for a second
+  row (a `<br>`, content past the rung, or text painted on two rows inside it), or
+  holds an interactive element, logs `[ToggleGroupItem] value=… asks for a second row
+  …` or `… holds an interactive descendant …`, at mount and whenever its content, its
+  classes or its inline styles change; a production build logs nothing. These checks
+  read `process.env.NODE_ENV`, which your bundler resolves (Vite and webpack do by
+  default).
+- `--selection-tile` (`none` | `flush` | `plated`, inherited) says whether a set of
+  options are tiles. It is `none` on every `ToggleGroup`, and nothing in the library
+  sets it yet. A value takes effect on the group element, or on an element between the
+  group and its options (a grid `div`, a `<section>` per family), whose options follow
+  it. On an ancestor of the group it has no effect, because every group resets it to
+  `none`. On an option or on content inside one it has no effect either: an option and
+  its content inherit the value above the option. An inline
+  `--selection-tile: … !important` on an option outranks that, and it does not opt the
+  option out: it splits it. The option keeps the corner and the column of the value
+  above it, which read the value on the option's parent, while its clip and the one-row
+  law, which read the option's own value, follow the inline write. Under either tile value an option is a
+  card, whatever wrote the value:
+  it is not held to one row, and it takes the field corner (16px), a column,
+  start-aligned text at body leading, the card pad (`--space-atom` block,
+  `--space-body` inline; `sm` `--space-residue` / `--space-atom`), the height of the
+  tallest option in its row, and its selected perimeter. A corner you give a tile by a
+  `rounded-*` utility, a scoped or unlayered rule, or an unlayered `!important` has no
+  effect; only an inline `border-radius: … !important` changes it. Under `flush` an
+  option also paints no plate, floor or blur at rest. A `ToggleGroup` nested inside a
+  tile is pills again (every group resets the value), and so is a `SegmentedTabs` strip
+  inside one (every strip resets it too).
+- The selected perimeter of a `ToggleGroup` item paints inside its 1px border instead
+  of straddling it, so paint containment (`content-visibility: auto`, the pill's own
+  clip) keeps all 2px of it.
+- A `ToggleGroup` item pops when it is selected, by pointer, key or a write to the
+  model: it peaks about 2.8 % over its rest (4.5 % under a loud driver,
+  `--motion-weight: 1`), bounces under it and settles, in 450 ms on `transform`. A calm
+  observer (`[data-reorder]`, `[data-autoplay]`) still pops at the rest amplitude;
+  reduced motion runs nothing. Tab entry and mount never pop. A consumer `transform` on
+  an item is overwritten while the pop runs.
+- A selected `ToggleGroup` item's label ink, tint and perimeter follow
+  `--selection-tone` (the label ink and the perimeter are the tone made solid and
+  floored to 3:1). `--selection-ink` is the item's ink (muted at rest, the selected
+  edge when on); media inside an item can stroke with it or with `currentColor` and
+  drop their own `[data-state="on"]` rules. A rest colour on an option's content is
+  deleted: the item's ink is already muted at rest, and content that sets its own
+  colour does not take the selected ink. Unset, every value is what it was.
+- Under `prefers-contrast: more` and forced colours a selected `ToggleGroup` item keeps
+  its 1px border and paints its own 2px perimeter layer, instead of widening its border
+  to 2px and moving its content.
+- Up and Down in a row of items of unequal height reach the item in the adjacent row,
+  not the diagonal one.
+- `--control-surface-bg-hover` is one flat mix of the foreground ink into `--card`:
+  at full glass the ink at the rest plate's alpha plus `--fill-hover`; under reduced
+  transparency an opaque `--card` with `--fill-hover` of the ink mixed in, so the hover
+  moves there too (it was the rest plate). The `.control-surface` hover (ToggleGroup
+  items, the Select trigger) paints it on the plate's own layer, only on a fine
+  pointer; the disclosure trigger's hover reads the same token.
+
